  "problem": "Given that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $",
  "worked_solution": "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}36{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}18{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
